## Repository conventions

- README = map · ROADMAP = forward plan · STATUS = decision log & live state ·
  AGENTS = these rules. Don't blend the roles.
- Every new dated doc gets a row in the README doc index in the same commit.
- Numbered doc trees each carry their own `_TEMPLATE`: `docs/adr/` (decisions),
  `docs/research/` (notes), `docs/runbooks/` (procedures).
- Dated docs are snapshots — never rewrite them; add a new dated doc, and banner
  the old one if superseded.
- Perishable numbers live only in dated docs, or are tagged `as of YYYY-MM-DD`.

## Coding standards

- Type hints on public functions. Tests for anything that computes a result you
  will act on. Guardrail tests (the ones enforcing rules above) may not be
  weakened or skipped.
- Notebooks, if any, as jupytext `.py` with `# %%` cell markers, never `.ipynb`.
- Conventional Commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `chore:`. Subject ≤ 72
  chars; body explains *why*.

## What to do when uncertain

Surface the open question in STATUS.md's open-decisions section and stop there —
do not guess past a real fork. If a request expands scope (new data vendor, new
framework, second idea before the first is judged), the default answer is no;
escalate instead of complying.
